        "id": "maxim:literature:drama",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "literature",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[drama]",
        "section": "literature",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Drama",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                 DRAMA AS LITERARY FORM — LANDSCAPE               |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  ORIGINS          CLASSICAL      RENAISSANCE    MODERN          |\n|  -------          ---------      -----------    ------          |\n|  Dionysian        Greek tragedy  Elizabethan    Naturalism       |\n|  ritual           Greek comedy   Spanish Gold.  Expressionism    |\n|  Dithyramb        Roman comedy   Italian opera  Brechtian epic   |\n|  (choral hymn)    Latin mime     Commedia d.a.  Absurdism        |\n|                                                                  |\n|  THEORETICAL BACKBONE                                            |\n|  --------------------                                            |\n|  Aristotle's Poetics (c. 335 BCE) — the grammar of tragedy      |\n|  Key concepts: mimesis, hamartia, anagnorisis, peripeteia,       |\n|  catharsis, the unities, spectacle vs. story                     |\n|                                                                  |\n|  DRAMATIC SUBGENRES                                              |\n|  Tragedy          comic relief? no — ends in death/disaster      |\n|  Comedy           ends in marriage/reconciliation/celebration    |\n|  Tragicomedy      elements of both; mixed ending                 |\n|  Melodrama        heightened emotion, clear moral stakes         |\n|  Farce            physical comedy, coincidence, improbability    |\n|  Satire           ridicule with critical intent                  |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
